mixin () {
  public query func getApiDoc() : async Text {
    "# LaNasa Booster — Backend API\n\n## Purpose\n\nThis canister stores per-user boost impulses, per-user Auto settings and per-user FF Performance Core settings for the LaNasa Booster app. Each signed-in user has an independent boost counter (`totalBoosts`), a power level (`powerLevel`), a history of recorded boosts, a saved set of Auto burst parameters, and a saved set of FF Performance Core tuning parameters. All state is scoped to the calling principal.\n\n## Authentication and identity\n\nEvery method derives the caller from the IC message principal. There is no anonymous access path: `recordBoost`, `resetBoosts`, `saveAutoSettings`, `resetAutoSettings`, `saveFfPerfSettings` and `resetFfPerfSettings` are update calls that write to the caller's own state, and `listBoosts`, `getBoostStats`, `getAutoSettings` and `getFfPerfSettings` are query calls that read only the caller's own state. A caller with no recorded boosts or saved settings receives defaults or empty results rather than an error.\n\nThe app's frontend pins an Internet Identity derivation origin, published at `/.well-known/ii-derivation-origin` when available. An agent already holding the user's Internet Identity authorization derives the correct per-app principal against that origin (for example `icp identity link web <name> --app <host>`). Such a delegation acts with the user's full authority in this app until it expires.\n\n## Authorization\n\nAll boost data, Auto settings and FF Performance settings are private to their owner. There is no admin or cross-user read path in the public API. The OQL `boost` entity is scoped per user: a signed-in caller sees only rows whose `owner` equals their principal, and the platform controller may read all rows for data-intelligence queries. The OQL `autoSettings` entity is likewise scoped per user: a signed-in caller sees only the row whose `owner` equals their principal, and the platform controller may read all rows for data-intelligence queries. The OQL `ffPerfSettings` entity is scoped per user in the same way: a signed-in caller sees only the row whose `owner` equals their principal, and the platform controller may read all rows for data-intelligence queries.\n\n## Methods\n\n### Boosts\n\n- `recordBoost() : async BoostStats` — appends one boost with the current time to the caller's history, increments `totalBoosts`, raises `powerLevel` by one, and returns the updated stats.\n- `listBoosts() : async [Boost]` — returns the caller's recorded boosts, newest first.\n- `getBoostStats() : async BoostStats` — returns the caller's `totalBoosts` and `powerLevel`.\n- `resetBoosts() : async BoostStats` — clears the caller's boost history and resets `totalBoosts` and `powerLevel` to zero.\n\n### Auto settings\n\n- `getAutoSettings() : async AutoSettings` — returns the caller's saved Auto settings, or the script defaults when none were saved. A read never creates an entry.\n- `saveAutoSettings(settings : AutoSettings) : async AutoSettings` — stores the caller's Auto settings and returns the stored value.\n- `resetAutoSettings() : async AutoSettings` — restores the caller's Auto settings to the script defaults and returns them.\n\n### FF Performance Core\n\n- `getFfPerfSettings() : async FfPerfSettings` — returns the caller's saved FF Performance Core settings, or the module defaults when none were saved. A read never creates an entry.\n- `saveFfPerfSettings(settings : FfPerfSettings) : async FfPerfSettings` — stores the caller's FF Performance Core settings and returns the stored value.\n- `resetFfPerfSettings() : async FfPerfSettings` — restores the caller's FF Performance Core settings to the module defaults and returns them.\n\n## Units and encoding\n\n- `Boost.timestamp` is an `Int` count of nanoseconds since the Unix epoch (IC `Time.now()`), not milliseconds or seconds.\n- `Boost.id` and every `Nat` field (`totalBoosts`, `powerLevel`, `targetFps`, and the Auto numeric fields) are unsigned integers; Candid transports them as arbitrary-precision naturals, so a JavaScript client receives them as `bigint`.\n- `FfPerfSettings` fields are: `targetFps : Nat` (target frames per second), `cpuGovernor : Text`, `ioScheduler : Text`, `networkMode : Text` and `thermalProfile : Text` (free-form option identifiers chosen by the UI).\n- The module defaults returned for an unsaved caller are `targetFps = 120`, `cpuGovernor = \"performance\"`, `ioScheduler = \"cfq\"`, `networkMode = \"balanced\"` and `thermalProfile = \"balanced\"`.\n- `owner` in the OQL rows is the caller's principal rendered as text.\n\n## Lifecycle and polling\n\n- `getFfPerfSettings`, `getAutoSettings`, `getBoostStats` and `listBoosts` are query calls: they are fast, unreplicated reads and never mutate state.\n- `saveFfPerfSettings` and `resetFfPerfSettings` are update calls. After a successful update the frontend should invalidate and refetch the corresponding query rather than poll; there is no server-side push or long-poll endpoint.\n- A caller that has never saved FF Performance settings always reads the defaults, so the UI can render immediately without a prior write.\n\n## Mutation retry safety\n\n- `saveFfPerfSettings` is idempotent: saving the same settings twice leaves the same stored value, so a retried call is safe.\n- `resetFfPerfSettings` is idempotent: it always writes the module defaults, so repeating it has no additional effect.\n- `recordBoost` is NOT idempotent: each successful call appends a new boost and increments the counters, so a retried call records a duplicate boost. Do not auto-retry it.\n- `resetBoosts` is destructive: it clears the caller's entire boost history. It is idempotent in effect but must not be retried blindly after an ambiguous failure.\n\n## Errors, traps and gotchas\n\n- No method traps on a missing record: an unsaved caller receives defaults or an empty list.\n- `saveFfPerfSettings` accepts any `FfPerfSettings` value; the backend does not validate the option strings, so the UI owns the allowed choices.\n- The OQL `schema()` and `execute()` endpoints are provided by the OQL `Expose` mixin and are subject to the per-entity authorization described above; they are not part of the app's own UI API.\n- `getApiDoc()` itself is a public query and returns this static document; it reads no state."
  };
};
